-- postpartum week 98: Zinc, and why a plant-heavy plate needs a little more of it (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   98,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Zinc, and why a plant-heavy plate needs a little more of it',
   '"Zinc supports your immune function, taste and wound repair. Meat, dairy and eggs give it up easily; legumes, nuts, seeds and whole grains contribute too, but less efficiently — how much you actually eat, and how well you absorb it, matter as much as variety does."',
   'Zinc supports your immune function, your sense of taste, and ongoing tissue repair. Meat, poultry, fish, eggs and dairy give it up most efficiently; legumes, nuts, seeds and whole grains contribute too, though the plant compounds that occur alongside zinc in those foods reduce how much your body actually absorbs from a given amount. That''s not a reason to avoid these foods — it''s a reason variety across your week matters more than any single food does. If your own diet has shifted toward more plant-based meals since earlier in this series, this is worth a little more deliberate attention now than it may have needed before. This week focuses on where your zinc is actually coming from, small ways to help your body use more of what plant sources offer, and when a persistent taste, appetite or healing change is worth raising with your doctor rather than waiting out.',
   'If you follow a vegetarian or vegan diet: spread legumes, nuts, seeds, whole grains and, if you eat them, dairy across your week rather than relying on just one or two — this is the main adjustment that matters here. If you have Crohn''s disease, ulcerative colitis, or another condition affecting nutrient absorption: your zinc needs and monitoring should follow your own care team''s specific guidance rather than this general week. If you''re considering a zinc supplement beyond an ordinary multivitamin: check the dose and how long to take it with your own doctor — a high dose taken long-term on your own can interfere with copper absorption. A persistent, unexplained change in taste or smell, an appetite that''s dropped off and stayed that way, or wounds that are healing more slowly than expected are worth raising with your own doctor. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, and are also efficiently absorbed zinc sources. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and zinc content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Punjabi cooking (aloo/gobi/mooli paratha, sarson da saag, chole, rajma, dal makhani, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Zinc supports your immune system, your sense of taste, and ongoing tissue repair. Meat, poultry, fish, eggs and dairy are the most efficiently absorbed sources; legumes, nuts, seeds and whole grains contribute meaningfully too, though the plant compounds that occur alongside zinc in those foods reduce how much your body actually absorbs from a given amount — not a reason to avoid them, just a reason variety across the day matters more than any one food.", "breakfast_a": "Aloo paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Tandoori chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pistachios (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "If you''ve cut back on meat", "notes": "A vegetarian diet can meet your zinc needs, but it takes a bit more deliberate attention — legumes, nuts, seeds, whole grains and dairy all contributing regularly, rather than leaning on just one or two of them. Include regular, meaningful portions of these foods; your needs, and how well they''re met, depend on your overall intake and absorption, not variety alone. A plant-heavy diet can call for particular attention to zinc bioavailability (NIH zinc guidance).", "breakfast_a": "Sarson da saag (1 cup) with makki di roti (2 pieces) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-mushroom toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chole (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Amritsari fish (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pomegranate", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with makhana, roasted fox nuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a dal makhani (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer tikka curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Soaking and sprouting can help a little", "notes": "Soaking, sprouting or fermenting legumes and grains before cooking modestly improves how much of their zinc your body can actually absorb — a small, genuine effect, worth knowing about if you already prepare food this way, but not something you need to start doing specifically for zinc.", "breakfast_a": "Chana chaat made with thoroughly cooked sprouted chana (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cheese toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Lobia curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Punjabi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a sweet lime", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Taste and appetite changes are worth mentioning", "notes": "A persistent, unexplained change in your sense of taste or smell, or an appetite that''s dropped off and stayed that way, is worth raising with your own doctor rather than something to just wait out — it can have several causes, of which low zinc is only one.", "breakfast_a": "Stuffed paratha, gobi (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-coriander sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kadhi pakora (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Punjabi-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with grapes", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a til-jaggery ladoo (1 piece)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "More isn''t automatically better", "notes": "A high-dose zinc supplement, taken on your own and for a long stretch, can actually interfere with how well your body absorbs copper — another nutrient you need. If you''re considering a zinc supplement beyond an ordinary multivitamin, check the dose and duration with your own doctor rather than assuming more is simply better.", "breakfast_a": "Missi roti (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Amritsari fish (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut muskmelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted chana (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-spinach curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one dish", "notes": "As with most nutrients in this series, no single meal needs to \"cover\" your zinc for the day — a varied plate of legumes, nuts, seeds, dairy and, if you eat them, meat or eggs across your week is what actually adds up, whether freshly cooked or safely reheated.", "breakfast_a": "Stuffed paratha, mooli (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-garlic omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chole (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Punjabi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an apple", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a chikki square, jaggery-walnut (1 piece)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Has your own diet leaned more plant-based lately — and if so, does your week actually include enough legumes, nuts, seeds and dairy, in real amounts, not just some variety on paper?", "breakfast_a": "Aloo paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Tandoori chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pistachios (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is there a small, ordinary food swap this week that would genuinely be easy to keep doing — not a big change, just one worth repeating?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another everyday nutrient worth a closer look — still grounded in the same idea: your own nourishment deserves the same attention as everyone else''s at the table.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 99: Iron, and why your needs may look different than right after birth (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   99,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Iron, and why your needs may look different than right after birth',
   '"Breastfeeding by itself isn''t a reason for higher iron needs — what actually matters is your own iron stores, whether your periods have returned, and whether you might be pregnant again. Your situation, not the calendar, decides where you fall."',
   'How much iron you actually need right now depends on your own iron stores, any ongoing bleeding, whether your periods have returned, and whether you might be pregnant again — not simply on how long ago you gave birth. Breastfeeding by itself isn''t a reason for higher iron needs — standard guidance for someone breastfeeding is actually lower than for a menstruating, non-pregnant adult. If your periods have returned, your needs may rise again with that regular monthly blood loss. If you''re pregnant again, needs rise further, and your prenatal team''s specific guidance takes over from this general week. Your own situation is what determines where you fall. This week focuses on haem versus non-haem iron sources, pairing non-haem sources with vitamin C for better absorption, and recognising when your own circumstances mean it''s worth a closer, individual look rather than a general assumption either way.',
   'If your periods have returned and are unusually heavy or prolonged: mention this to your own doctor — it can meaningfully raise your iron needs and is worth checking rather than assuming it''s simply normal. If you''re pregnant again: iron needs rise further on top of your current baseline — follow your prenatal team''s specific guidance on iron rather than this general week''s pattern. If you''re planning a pregnancy: trying to conceive doesn''t by itself raise your iron requirement to pregnancy levels — review your current iron status and any supplement needs with your doctor rather than automatically increasing iron in advance. Pregnancy itself raises requirements; treating an existing deficiency is a separate matter (NIH iron guidance). If you take a prescribed iron supplement: take it exactly as prescribed. Tea and coffee can reduce absorption, so a roughly two-hour gap from tea or coffee is commonly advised — confirm the specific timing with your prescriber, since it''s a separate interaction from any calcium-related timing you may have heard about. Persistent fatigue, unusually pale skin, or breathlessness with ordinary activity are worth a clinical assessment, with tests if indicated, rather than assuming iron deficiency (or ruling it out) on your own. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here — meat, poultry and fish give you haem iron, and eggs a non-haem source. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Tamil and Chettinad cooking (idli, sambar, kuzhambu varieties, poriyal, meen kuzhambu, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "How much iron you need right now depends on your own iron stores, any ongoing bleeding, whether your periods have returned, and whether you might be pregnant again — not simply on how long ago you gave birth. Breastfeeding by itself isn''t a reason for higher iron needs; standard guidance for someone breastfeeding is actually lower than for a menstruating, non-pregnant adult. Your own situation is what determines where you fall, not a fixed rule tied to your baby''s age.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-beetroot toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kara kuzhambu, tamarind lentil curry (3/4 cup, with 1/2 cup cooked toor dal per serving) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tomato poriyal", "lunch_b": "Chettinad chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a poriyal", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with raisins (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg kuzhambu, egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Haem and non-haem iron are absorbed differently", "notes": "Meat, poultry and fish carry haem iron, which your body absorbs relatively easily. Eggs, legumes, leafy greens and fortified grains carry non-haem iron, which your body absorbs less efficiently — eggs are a non-haem source too, despite being an animal food. Pairing a non-haem source with a vitamin C food in the same meal — citrus, tomato, amla — genuinely helps your body absorb more of it.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi koozh, fermented millet porridge (1 bowl) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-methi toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kootu, lentil-vegetable curry (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lemon wedge", "lunch_b": "Meen kuzhambu, fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lemon wedge", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a kiwi", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with dried figs (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rasam-paruppu (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "If your periods have returned", "notes": "Once your periods return, your iron needs may actually rise again, not fall — regular monthly blood loss is itself a meaningful factor. If your periods are unusually heavy or prolonged, that''s worth mentioning to your own doctor rather than assuming it''s simply normal for you.", "breakfast_a": "Appam (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cabbage sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Puli kuzhambu, tamarind vegetable curry (3/4 cup) with a moong dal (1/2 cup), rice (1 cup cooked) and a poriyal", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Chettinad-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a poriyal", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a sitaphal, custard apple", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with pine nuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a keerai kootu, spinach-lentil curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-spinach curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you''re pregnant again, or thinking about it", "notes": "Pregnancy itself raises iron needs further, on top of whatever your current baseline is — if you''re pregnant again, your prenatal team''s specific guidance on iron takes over from this general week. Trying to conceive doesn''t by itself raise your iron requirement to pregnancy levels; it''s a good time to review your current iron status and any supplement needs with your doctor, rather than assuming you should increase iron in advance.", "breakfast_a": "Idiyappam, string hoppers (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-paneer bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Sambar (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a poriyal", "lunch_b": "Kozhi milagu varuval, pepper chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a poriyal", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with jamun", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a chikki square, jaggery-peanut (1 piece)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a paruppu urundai kuzhambu, lentil-dumpling curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If you take an iron supplement", "notes": "If your doctor has you on an iron supplement, take it exactly as prescribed — don''t adjust the dose or schedule yourself. Tea and coffee can reduce how well an iron supplement absorbs, so a gap of about two hours from tea or coffee is a commonly used guideline; check the specific timing with whoever prescribed it, since it can differ from other timing advice you may have heard.", "breakfast_a": "Pongal (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-chilli omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kara kuzhambu (3/4 cup, with 1/2 cup cooked toor dal per serving) with rice (1 cup cooked), a cooked vegetable and a lemon wedge", "lunch_b": "Meen kuzhambu, fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with litchi", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted flaxseed stirred in (1 tbsp)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a horse gram curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one dish", "notes": "As with the other nutrients this series has covered, no single dish needs to carry your iron for the day — a varied plate across your week, with a vitamin C food alongside your non-haem sources when you can, is what actually adds up.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi koozh (1 bowl) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-carrot bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kootu (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tomato-onion poriyal", "lunch_b": "Chicken chukka, Tamil-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a poriyal", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a mango", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a chikki square, jaggery-mixed nuts (1 piece)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a kulthi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Which of the four factors this week named — your own stores, ongoing bleeding, whether periods have returned, a possible pregnancy — actually applies to you right now?", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-beetroot toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kara kuzhambu, tamarind lentil curry (3/4 cup, with 1/2 cup cooked toor dal per serving) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tomato poriyal", "lunch_b": "Chettinad chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a poriyal", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with raisins (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg kuzhambu, egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Has anything about your own body''s rhythm — periods, appetite, energy — shifted lately in a way that''s worth mentioning at your next check-up, even if it feels minor?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another everyday theme — still grounded in the same idea: your own nourishment deserves the same attention as everyone else''s at the table.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
