-- postpartum week 100: How much do you actually need right now? Trusting your own appetite (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   100,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How much do you actually need right now? Trusting your own appetite',
   '"There''s no single portion size that''s right for everyone, or even for you every single day. Hunger and fullness cues are usually a more useful everyday guide than counting or comparing to anyone else''s plate."',
   'How much food you actually need can genuinely vary day to day — with activity, sleep, stress, and how often you''re currently breastfeeding. There''s no single portion size that''s right for everyone, or even for you every single day. Hunger and fullness cues are usually a more useful everyday guide than counting or comparing to anyone else''s plate. If you''re breastfeeding less often now than earlier, or have stopped, it''s common for your appetite to settle to something closer to how it felt before pregnancy — an ordinary adjustment, not something to resist or hurry along. Smaller, more frequent eating and safely reheated leftovers nourish you just as genuinely as a sit-down plated meal; the aim is enough food across your day, not a particular plate size or eating style. If hunger cues are muted by stress, illness or medication, regular meals and your individual care plan can help you eat enough. This week focuses on noticing your own hunger and fullness, recognising that appetite naturally shifts as breastfeeding changes, and being gentle with yourself either way about what a weight-related goal — or the choice not to have one — means for you.',
   'If you have diabetes, prediabetes, or are managing blood sugar from a pregnancy that involved gestational diabetes: follow your own prescribed portion and carbohydrate targets — those take precedence over this week''s general portion guide. The A and B options each day aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which specific dishes fit your own targets rather than assuming either is interchangeable. If eating enough, or eating in a way that feels controlled by anxiety rather than hunger, has been a struggle for you: a conversation with your own doctor or a registered dietitian is a supportive next step, not a sign of failure. If a persistent loss of appetite, or eating that feels distressing or out of your control, has been going on for a while: raise it with your own doctor — this can have several causes, some of them worth addressing directly rather than waiting out. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite, which is the whole point of this particular week. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti or bhakri is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Maharashtrian cooking (poha, thalipeeth, varan bhaat, amti, bhakri, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "How much food you actually need can genuinely vary day to day — with activity, sleep, stress, and how often you''re currently breastfeeding. There''s no single portion size that''s right for everyone, or even for you every single day. Hunger and fullness cues are usually a more useful everyday guide than counting or comparing to anyone else''s plate.", "breakfast_a": "Poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-corn toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Varan (3/4 cup dal) with cooked rice (1 cup) and a bharli vangi, stuffed eggplant", "lunch_b": "Kolhapuri chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bharli vangi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut pineapple", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with soaked almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Bhakri (2 pieces) with an amti (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with bhakri (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "As breastfeeding changes, appetite often changes too", "notes": "If you''re breastfeeding less often now than earlier — or have stopped — it''s common for your appetite to settle to something closer to how it felt before pregnancy. That''s an ordinary adjustment, not a sign anything is wrong, and it doesn''t need to be resisted or hurried along.", "breakfast_a": "Thalipeeth (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-basil sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Matki usal, sprouted moth bean curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken sukka (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a dragon fruit", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a chikki square, jaggery-almond (1 piece)", "dinner_a": "Bhakri (2 pieces) with a pitla, gram-flour curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with bhakri (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Smaller, more frequent eating nourishes you too", "notes": "A quick bite eaten standing up, or leftovers reheated and eaten straight from the container, nourish you just as genuinely as a sit-down plated meal. The aim is enough food across your day — protein, fibre, and everything else — not a particular plate size or eating style.", "breakfast_a": "Sabudana khichdi (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-dill toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kothimbir vadi, coriander-gram fritters (4 pieces) with varan (3/4 cup dal), rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Bombil fry, Bombay duck fish (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with dried apricots (15g)", "dinner_a": "Bhakri (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with bhakri (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Noticing why you''re reaching for food, gently", "notes": "Eating from genuine hunger and eating to cope with stress or exhaustion are both completely normal parts of being human — noticing the difference, without judging yourself for either, can simply help you understand your own patterns a little better.", "breakfast_a": "Misal pav (1 bowl) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-peas omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Varan (3/4 cup dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bharli vangi", "lunch_b": "Kolhapuri mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bharli vangi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted watermelon seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Bhakri (2 pieces) with a matki usal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with bhakri (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "A weight-related goal, or none at all, are both fine", "notes": "Whether you want to work toward a particular weight or shape, or you''d rather not set that kind of goal right now, both are completely reasonable choices — wanting a goal isn''t something to feel embarrassed about, any more than not wanting one is.", "breakfast_a": "Zunka bhakri (1 cup zunka with 2 bhakri) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-potato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Pitla (3/4 cup) with bhakri (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Maharashtrian-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pistachios (15g)", "dinner_a": "Bhakri (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with bhakri (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Building trust in your own hunger, one week at a time", "notes": "This kind of trust builds gradually, not all at once — noticing one small, manageable moment this week where you ate because you were genuinely hungry, or stopped because you felt satisfied, is enough.", "breakfast_a": "Puran poli (1 piece) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-olive toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Varan (3/4 cup dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kothimbir vadi (4 pieces)", "lunch_b": "Kolhapuri chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pomegranate", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with makhana, roasted fox nuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Bhakri (2 pieces) with a pitla (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with bhakri (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Looking back on this week, did your appetite actually stay the same every day — or did it move around more than you usually give it credit for?", "breakfast_a": "Poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-corn toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Varan (3/4 cup dal) with cooked rice (1 cup) and a bharli vangi, stuffed eggplant", "lunch_b": "Kolhapuri chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bharli vangi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut pineapple", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with soaked almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Bhakri (2 pieces) with an amti (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with bhakri (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is there a rule about eating — a number, a timing, a plate size — that you''ve been holding yourself to without ever really deciding it was the right one for you?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another everyday nutrient worth a closer look — still grounded in the same idea: your own nourishment deserves the same attention as everyone else''s at the table.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 101: Vitamin C, for reasons beyond the healing phase now (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   101,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Vitamin C, for reasons beyond the healing phase now',
   '"Vitamin C remains important for collagen formation, tissue maintenance, immune function and absorption of non-haem iron — not only during early recovery, but as an ongoing everyday need."',
   'Vitamin C remains important for collagen formation, tissue maintenance, immune function and absorption of non-haem iron — not only during early recovery, but as an ongoing everyday need (NIH vitamin C guidance). Citrus fruit, amla, guava, tomato and capsicum are all good everyday sources. Pairing a vitamin C food with a non-haem iron source — dal, legumes, leafy greens, fortified grains — genuinely helps your body absorb more of that iron, which connects directly to Week 99''s iron focus. Vitamin C also breaks down with heat and over time once food is cut, so freshly cut fruit and lightly cooked vegetables generally retain more of it than food left sitting or heavily overcooked — not a reason to eat everything raw, just worth keeping in mind. This week focuses on everyday vitamin C sources, pairing them usefully with iron-rich meals, and a couple of situations — smoking, sensitive teeth — where it''s worth a little individual attention.',
   'If you have reflux or heartburn that''s triggered by acidic food: citrus and tomato-based dishes may worsen symptoms for you specifically — choose the non-citrus, non-tomato option on those days and season conservatively. If you smoke: your vitamin C needs are somewhat higher, since smoking increases how quickly your body uses it up — and if you''re considering quitting, your own doctor can point you toward support that actually helps. If you''re considering a vitamin C supplement beyond an ordinary multivitamin: check the dose with your own doctor first — very high doses can contribute to kidney stones in some people and can interfere with certain lab tests. If you have a history of kidney stones: mention any vitamin C supplement use to your doctor, since this is one of several factors worth reviewing together. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Awadhi and Lucknowi cooking (sheermal, roomali roti, dum aloo, korma varieties, galouti kebab, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Vitamin C remains important for collagen formation, tissue maintenance, immune function and absorption of non-haem iron — not only during early recovery, but as an ongoing everyday need. Citrus fruit, amla, guava, tomato and capsicum are all good everyday sources.", "breakfast_a": "Sheermal (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-lettuce sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dum aloo (1 cup) with a chana dal (1/2 cup), rice (1 cup cooked) and a tomato-capsicum salad", "lunch_b": "Murgh korma (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tomato-capsicum salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roomali roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg korma (2 eggs) with roomali roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Pairing vitamin C with your iron-rich meals", "notes": "Having a vitamin C food alongside a non-haem iron source — dal, legumes, leafy greens, fortified grains — genuinely helps your body absorb more of that iron. A squeeze of lemon, a tomato-based curry, or a side of citrus with an iron-focused meal is a simple, useful pairing, echoing Week 99''s iron focus.", "breakfast_a": "Ulte tawa ka paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-paprika toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Lauki chana dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lemon wedge", "lunch_b": "Galouti kebab (100g mutton mince) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lemon wedge", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roomali roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer korma (100g paneer) with roomali roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Freshness affects how much vitamin C survives cooking", "notes": "Vitamin C breaks down with heat and over time once food is cut — freshly cut fruit and lightly cooked vegetables generally keep more of it than food that''s been sitting cut for a while or is heavily overcooked. This isn''t a reason to eat everything raw, just worth keeping in mind when you can.", "breakfast_a": "Tehri, vegetable pulao (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-fenugreek omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dum aloo (1 cup) with a moong dal (1/2 cup), rice (1 cup cooked) and a fresh cucumber-tomato salad", "lunch_b": "Fish korma (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a fresh cucumber-tomato salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with fresh amla (3-4 pieces)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pine nuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roomali roti (2 pieces) with a lobia curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-spinach curry (2 eggs) with roomali roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you smoke", "notes": "If you smoke, your vitamin C needs are somewhat higher than someone who doesn''t, since smoking increases how quickly your body uses it up. If you''re thinking about quitting, your own doctor can point you toward support that actually helps — this week''s menu doesn''t change that conversation, just worth flagging that it''s one worth having.", "breakfast_a": "Khasta kachori (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-radish bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana masala (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tomato-capsicum salad", "lunch_b": "Chicken korma, mild (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cucumber raita, small bowl", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a sweet lime", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roomali roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roomali roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Citrus and sensitive teeth", "notes": "If your teeth feel sensitive to acidic food, citrus and amla can sting a little — rinsing your mouth with plain water afterwards is a simple habit that helps. If a food causes discomfort, choose another vitamin-C source you tolerate, such as capsicum or lightly cooked broccoli. Persistent sensitivity deserves dental advice.", "breakfast_a": "Kulcha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-zucchini toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dum aloo (1 cup) with a masoor dal (1/2 cup), rice (1 cup cooked) and a capsicum-tomato salad", "lunch_b": "Galouti kebab (100g mutton mince) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a capsicum-tomato salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with dried figs (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roomali roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-tomato korma (2 eggs) with roomali roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one dish", "notes": "As with the rest of this series, a vitamin C food showing up regularly across your week — not perfectly, not at every meal — is what actually matters, whether that''s fresh fruit, a tomato-based curry, or a squeeze of lemon on your dal.", "breakfast_a": "Tehri (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-broccoli toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Arhar dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lemon wedge", "lunch_b": "Murgh korma (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lemon wedge", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an apple", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a chikki square, jaggery-cashew (1 piece)", "dinner_a": "Roomali roti (2 pieces) with a lobia-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roomali roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Did you notice yourself pairing a vitamin C food with an iron-rich meal this week — even once, even without trying very hard?", "breakfast_a": "Sheermal (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-lettuce sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dum aloo (1 cup) with a chana dal (1/2 cup), rice (1 cup cooked) and a tomato-capsicum salad", "lunch_b": "Murgh korma (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tomato-capsicum salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roomali roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg korma (2 eggs) with roomali roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Which everyday food on your own plate is quietly doing more nutritional work than you usually give it credit for?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another everyday nutrient worth a closer look — still grounded in the same idea: your own nourishment deserves the same attention as everyone else''s at the table.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
