-- postpartum week 102: Omega-3s for your own ongoing nourishment (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   102,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Omega-3s for your own ongoing nourishment',
   '"Omega-3 fats remain part of your own nourishment, whether you breastfeed or not — especially EPA and DHA, which support your heart and your brain''s ongoing health, and are one of several everyday factors some research links to mood. Fish gives you ready-made EPA and DHA directly; plant sources give a form your body converts only partly."',
   'Omega-3 fats remain part of your own nourishment, whether you breastfeed or not. Especially EPA and DHA, they support your heart and your brain''s ongoing health, and are one of several everyday factors some research links to mood. Fish and seafood are the most direct source of ready-made EPA and DHA. If you don''t eat fish, plant sources like flaxseed, walnuts and chia give a different form (ALA), which your body converts to EPA and DHA only in small amounts — genuinely worth including regularly either way, but not a complete substitute for fish if you eat it. This week focuses on everyday omega-3 sources, a fish-oil supplement caution worth knowing if you take blood-thinning medication, and keeping mood-related claims realistic rather than overstated.',
   'If you take a blood-thinning medication, or are scheduled for a procedure: mention any fish oil or omega-3 supplement you take to your doctor — a high dose can have a mild blood-thinning effect of its own. If you follow a vegetarian or vegan diet, or otherwise don''t eat fish: flaxseed, walnuts and chia are worth including regularly, and it''s worth discussing with your doctor whether an algae-based omega-3 supplement is right for you, since plant ALA converts only partially to EPA and DHA. If you''re dealing with persistent low mood or anxiety: raise it with your own doctor regardless of what your diet already looks like — omega-3 is one of several everyday factors linked to mood in some research, not a substitute for treatment (NCCIH evidence summary). These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, and fish is also the most direct omega-3 source. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Assamese cooking (til pitha, khar, masor tenga, bilahi maas, khorisa dal, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Omega-3 fats remain part of your own nourishment, whether you breastfeed or not — especially EPA and DHA, which support your heart and your brain''s ongoing health, and are one of several everyday factors some research links to mood. Fish and seafood are the most direct source of ready-made EPA and DHA; if you don''t eat fish, plant sources like flaxseed, walnuts and chia give a different form (ALA) that your body converts to EPA and DHA only in small amounts.", "breakfast_a": "Til pitha, sesame rice cake (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-herb sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a khar, raw papaya and alkaline curry", "lunch_b": "Masor tenga, sour fish curry made with sardines (100g sardines, an FDA \"Best Choices\" oily fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a khar", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut pineapple", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a matar dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Fish is the most direct source — choose thoughtfully", "notes": "If you eat fish, Atlantic mackerel and sardines are both on the FDA/EPA \"Best Choices\" list — good everyday omega-3 sources that also keep mercury exposure low. The species matters here: Atlantic mackerel is a \"Best Choice,\" while king mackerel is one the FDA advises avoiding altogether. Two to three servings a week, from varied fish, is a commonly used guideline.", "breakfast_a": "Pitha, rice cake (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-ginger omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pitika, mashed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Bilahi maas, tomato Atlantic mackerel curry (100g Atlantic mackerel, an FDA \"Best Choices\" oily fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pitika", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with jamun", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with raisins (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Plant sources still count, just differently", "notes": "Walnuts, flaxseed and chia give ALA, a plant omega-3 — genuinely worth including regularly, but your body converts only a small fraction of it into the EPA and DHA your heart and brain use directly. If you don''t eat fish at all, this is worth discussing with your doctor, including whether an algae-based omega-3 supplement makes sense for you.", "breakfast_a": "Chira, flattened rice (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-scallion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khorisa dal, bamboo shoot dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Assamese chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with ground flaxseed stirred in (1 tbsp)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lobia curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you take a blood thinner, or an omega-3 supplement", "notes": "If you''re on a blood-thinning medication, or scheduled for a procedure, mention any fish oil or omega-3 supplement you take to your doctor — a high dose can have a mild blood-thinning effect of its own, which matters alongside a prescribed medication.", "breakfast_a": "Komal chawal pitha, soft rice pitha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-sesame toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a khar", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Assamese-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a khar", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a mango", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a chikki square, jaggery-almond (1 piece)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "A useful piece of a bigger picture for your mood", "notes": "Omega-3s are one of several everyday factors linked to mood in some research (NCCIH evidence summary) — genuinely worth including, but not a substitute for treatment if you''re dealing with persistent low mood or anxiety. That''s worth raising with your own doctor regardless of what your diet already looks like.", "breakfast_a": "Pitha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-pepper toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khorisa dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pitika", "lunch_b": "Assamese chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pitika", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a sitaphal, custard apple", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few soaked chia seeds (1 tbsp)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-tomato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one dish", "notes": "As with the rest of this series, the goal is omega-3 showing up regularly across your week — a fish meal if you eat fish, a small daily handful of walnuts or a spoon of flaxseed if you don''t — not a single perfect food doing all the work.", "breakfast_a": "Bhaat pitha, rice pitha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-avocado toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Masoor dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a khar", "lunch_b": "Assamese chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a khar", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with litchi", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a chikki square, jaggery-flaxseed (1 piece)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a kulthi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. This brings the week''s fish total to three, still within the usual 2-3/week guideline. Thinking about your own food habits this week — did omega-3 actually show up in what you ate, or did it quietly slip down the list?", "breakfast_a": "Til pitha, sesame rice cake (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-herb sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a khar, raw papaya and alkaline curry", "lunch_b": "Masor tenga, sour fish curry made with sardines (100g sardines, an FDA \"Best Choices\" oily fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a khar", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut pineapple", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a matar dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Looking back across this whole batch of weeks — B12, zinc, iron, appetite, vitamin C, omega-3 — which one genuinely felt like it was about YOU, maybe for the first time in a while?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with a new stretch of everyday themes — still grounded in the same idea: your own nourishment deserves the same attention as everyone else''s at the table.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 103: Vitamin A, where more isn't automatically better (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   103,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Vitamin A, where more isn''t automatically better',
   '"Vitamin A supports your vision, immune function and skin — but this is one nutrient where more isn''t automatically better. The preformed kind, from animal foods and supplements, has a meaningful upper limit; the plant kind, from colourful fruit and vegetables, doesn''t carry the same risk."',
   'Vitamin A supports your vision, immune function and skin. It comes in two different forms that behave quite differently in your body: preformed vitamin A (retinol), from animal foods and supplements, which your body stores and can build up to harmful levels if intake stays very high over time; and provitamin A carotenoids, from colourful fruit and vegetables, which your body converts only as much of as it needs. This distinction is why the usual advice here runs differently from most other nutrients in this series — for provitamin A, more colourful produce is simply fine; for preformed vitamin A, a supplement or liver is where it''s worth a closer look, not only when portions are frequent or large — and if you''re pregnant again or trying to conceive, liver is best avoided altogether, not just kept occasional. This week focuses on where each form of vitamin A comes from, why liver is worth keeping occasional generally (and avoiding entirely during pregnancy), and situations — a new pregnancy, a retinoid medication — where it''s worth checking your total intake with your own doctor.',
   'If you''re pregnant again, or trying to conceive: high-dose preformed vitamin A has been linked to a higher risk of certain birth defects — avoid liver and liver products altogether while pregnant, and review the vitamin A content and form on any prenatal or multivitamin label, or any supplement containing retinol, with your maternity team (NHS pregnancy vitamins and supplements guidance). If you take a prescribed retinoid medication, such as for acne or another skin condition: mention any additional vitamin A supplement you''re considering to your prescribing doctor before starting it — dietary and medication sources of vitamin A don''t simply add together as one intake number, and combining them can increase the risk of adverse effects. If you have chronic liver disease: your liver stores vitamin A, so your own doctor''s specific guidance on supplementation and liver-rich foods takes precedence over this general week. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content — your own prescribed meal plan or hypoglycaemia guidance takes precedence over these general suggestions. Persistent headache, nausea, blurred vision or unusual hair loss alongside heavy supplement use is worth a clinical assessment rather than assuming it''s unrelated. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and vitamin A content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Kerala and Malabar cooking (puttu, appam, avial, olan, thoran, meen moilee, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Vitamin A comes in two different forms. Preformed vitamin A (retinol) is in animal foods — liver, egg yolk, dairy — and in supplements; your body stores it, and too much over time can build up to harmful levels. Provitamin A carotenoids are in colourful fruit and vegetables — carrot, pumpkin, sweet potato, mango, spinach; your body converts only what it needs into usable vitamin A, so eating plenty of these foods doesn''t carry the same overload risk.", "breakfast_a": "Puttu (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with appam (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Avial, mixed vegetable coconut curry (1 cup, with carrot and pumpkin) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a paruppu, moong dal (1/2 cup)", "lunch_b": "Meen moilee, coconut fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a thoran", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a cherupayar curry, green gram dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Preformed vitamin A — a little goes a long way", "notes": "Liver is an especially concentrated source of preformed vitamin A — a small, occasional portion is plenty for most people; eating it often, or in large amounts, is the main everyday way this nutrient can build up too high. If you''re pregnant again, avoid liver and liver products altogether rather than just keeping them occasional — this is a pregnancy-specific caution, not a general everyday one. Egg yolk and dairy contribute preformed vitamin A too, but in much smaller, steadier amounts that don''t raise the same concern at ordinary intakes.", "breakfast_a": "Idiyappam, string hoppers (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Olan, ash gourd and coconut milk curry (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cherupayar curry", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Malabar-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a thoran", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a parippu, red lentil curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "The plant form works differently", "notes": "Carrot, pumpkin, sweet potato, mango, papaya and leafy greens all carry provitamin A carotenoids in generous amounts. Cooking and a little fat — the coconut oil or ghee already common in these meals — actually help your body absorb more of it. There''s no everyday need to limit how much of these particular foods you eat for this reason.", "breakfast_a": "Kappa, tapioca (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Mathanga erissery, pumpkin and lentil curry (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a thoran", "lunch_b": "Nadan meen curry, home-style fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a thoran", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a slice of mango", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a cherupayar curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-carrot curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you''re pregnant again, or your supplement includes vitamin A", "notes": "High-dose preformed vitamin A — from a supplement or from liver — has been linked to a higher risk of certain birth defects when taken in early pregnancy. If you''re pregnant again or trying to conceive, avoid liver and liver products altogether, and review the vitamin A content and form on any prenatal or multivitamin label with your maternity team (NHS pregnancy vitamins and supplements guidance). This caution is specific to the preformed, animal-and-supplement form; carotenoids from fruit and vegetables don''t carry it.", "breakfast_a": "Pathiri (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Thoran, cabbage and coconut stir-fry (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a parippu", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Malabar-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a thoran", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a mathanga curry, pumpkin curry (1/2 cup) and a measured 1/2 cup cooked cherupayar (green gram)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If you take a retinoid medication or a vitamin A supplement", "notes": "If you''re on a prescribed retinoid — for acne or another skin condition — mention any additional vitamin A supplement you''re considering to your prescribing doctor. Dietary and medication vitamin A aren''t simply interchangeable intake amounts, and combining the two can increase the risk of adverse effects rather than just adding to a total. Persistent headache, nausea, blurred vision or unusual hair loss with heavy supplement use is worth a clinical assessment rather than assuming it''s unrelated.", "breakfast_a": "Kallappam, rice pancake (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-mushroom bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Cherupayar curry, green gram dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beetroot thoran", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Malabar-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beetroot thoran", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a sweet lime", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an olan, ash gourd and val beans curry (3/4 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked val beans)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one dish", "notes": "As with the rest of this series, no single meal needs to carry your vitamin A for the day — a regular mix of colourful vegetables and fruit, with dairy or egg contributing steadily and liver kept occasional if you eat it (avoided altogether if you''re pregnant), is what actually adds up over your week.", "breakfast_a": "Neer dosa (3 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-coriander toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Parippu, red lentil curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot thoran", "lunch_b": "Chicken chukka, Malabar-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot thoran", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a slice of papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a chikki square, jaggery-cashew (1 piece)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a mathanga erissery (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. This brings the week''s fish total to three, still within the usual 2-3/week guideline. Thinking back over this week — is liver, or a vitamin A supplement, actually part of your own routine, or was this mostly about the colourful vegetables you already eat?", "breakfast_a": "Puttu (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with appam (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Avial, mixed vegetable coconut curry (1 cup, with carrot and pumpkin) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a paruppu, moong dal (1/2 cup)", "lunch_b": "Meen moilee, coconut fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a thoran", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a cherupayar curry, green gram dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is there a supplement or multivitamin in your own cabinet whose label you''ve never actually read closely?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another everyday theme — still grounded in the same idea: your own nourishment deserves the same attention as everyone else''s at the table.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
