-- postpartum week 104: Two years in — checking in on your own eating (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   104,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Two years in — checking in on your own eating',
   '"Your own nourishment has mattered every day of these last two years, and that still doesn''t end today. Whatever your routine looks like now is a reasonable place to be."',
   'Around two years past birth, a lot has likely shifted — sleep, appetite, daily rhythm, maybe your family''s plans too. This week is a check-in rather than a new topic: a look back at the nutrients this series has flagged along the way, and an honest look at which habits actually stuck. Whatever your situation is right now — still breastfeeding, weaned long ago, expecting again, somewhere else entirely — is exactly right for you. There''s no single point everyone is supposed to have reached by two years. This week focuses on revisiting iron, calcium, vitamin D, B12 and protein in passing rather than in depth, noticing which of this series'' habits genuinely lasted, and what a new pregnancy — if that''s part of your picture — would mean for revisiting earlier guidance.',
   'If you''re pregnant again, or trying to conceive: your prenatal team''s specific guidance on iron, folate, vitamin A and any medication timing takes over from this general series — revisit these individually rather than from memory. If you''re still breastfeeding at two years: WHO recommends continued breastfeeding alongside a varied diet for up to two years of age or beyond, for as long as it works for you and your child — there''s no automatic reason to stop at any particular point. If your appetite, energy or weight has shifted in a way that concerns you: a conversation with your own doctor is a reasonable next step, especially if the change has been persistent rather than day-to-day. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content — your own prescribed meal plan or hypoglycaemia guidance takes precedence over these general suggestions. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Goan cooking (sanna, poee, dalitoy, khatkhate, xacuti, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Two years into this series, the idea hasn''t changed: whatever today''s plate looks like, feeding yourself still matters exactly as much as it did in week one. There''s no finish line here, and reaching this week doesn''t mean you''re supposed to have \"arrived\" anywhere in particular.", "breakfast_a": "Sanna (3 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with poee (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalitoy, tempered toor dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tondak", "lunch_b": "Chicken xacuti (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tondak", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "If your appetite or routine has settled into something new", "notes": "By two years, many mothers find their appetite, sleep and daily rhythm have settled into a pattern that looks quite different from the early postpartum stretch — sometimes calmer, sometimes just differently busy. Whatever your own routine looks like now is a reasonable starting point, not something to measure against anyone else''s.", "breakfast_a": "Poee (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khatkhate, mixed vegetable and lentil stew (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Ambot tik, sour-hot fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a chikoo", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "A quick check-in on the nutrients this series has flagged", "notes": "This series has pointed to a handful of nutrients worth ongoing attention — iron, calcium, vitamin D, B12 and protein among them. None of them need a dramatic overhaul today; a brief, honest check — am I still getting a dependable source of each across most weeks — is enough. If something feels like it''s slipped for a while, that''s worth mentioning at your next check-up rather than a big change made alone.", "breakfast_a": "Sanna (3 pieces) with a tondak and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-coconut bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalitoy (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans foogath, coconut stir-fry", "lunch_b": "Mutton xacuti (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans foogath", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut pineapple", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you''re pregnant again, or your family is still growing", "notes": "If you''re expecting again, or thinking about it, many of the specific cautions from earlier in this series — iron, folate, vitamin A, medication timing — are worth revisiting with your prenatal team rather than assumed from memory. Two years'' distance from your last pregnancy doesn''t change that your next one, if there is one, deserves its own fresh look.", "breakfast_a": "Patoleo, rice dumplings (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-chilli omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Tondak, vegetable and lentil curry (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage foogath", "lunch_b": "Chicken cafreal (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage foogath", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Noticing what actually stuck", "notes": "Some habits from this series probably stuck without much effort — a favourite quick breakfast, a nourishment-break pairing you still reach for. Others quietly dropped away, and that''s fine too; not everything needs to last forever to have been useful when it mattered.", "breakfast_a": "Poee (2 pieces) with a khatkhate and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-coriander sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalitoy (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beetroot foogath", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Goan-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beetroot foogath", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a matar dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-spinach curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one dish", "notes": "As with every week in this series, there''s no single meal or day that needs to prove anything. Two years on, the same basic idea holds: regular food, enough of it, and room to adjust — for you, not just for everyone else at your table.", "breakfast_a": "Sanna (3 pieces) with a beans foogath and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-potato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Tondak (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot foogath", "lunch_b": "Chicken xacuti (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot foogath", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a kiwi", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a chikki square, jaggery-cashew (1 piece)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Of everything this series has covered in two years, what''s the one thing about your own eating you''d actually want to keep doing?", "breakfast_a": "Sanna (3 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with poee (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalitoy, tempered toor dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tondak", "lunch_b": "Chicken xacuti (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tondak", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'If a friend two years behind you asked for one piece of advice about feeding herself, not her baby, what would you tell her?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another everyday theme — still grounded in the same idea: your own nourishment deserves the same attention as everyone else''s at the table.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 105: Choline, a nutrient easy to overlook in your own diet (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   105,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Choline, a nutrient easy to overlook in your own diet',
   '"Choline supports your liver function, your cell membranes and your nervous system. It doesn''t get much everyday attention, but most diets — especially plant-heavy ones — run light on it without anyone noticing."',
   'Choline supports your liver function, the structure of your cell membranes, and your nervous system (NIH choline guidance). It''s not a nutrient that comes up often, which is part of why diets — especially plant-heavy ones — can run light on it without anyone noticing. Egg yolk, meat, fish and dairy are the richest everyday sources; soy foods, legumes and cruciferous vegetables — broccoli, cauliflower, cabbage — contribute smaller but real amounts. If your own diet leans plant-based, this is one where deliberate variety across your week matters more than it does for many other nutrients this series has covered. This week focuses on where choline actually comes from, why egg yolk is worth keeping in the picture if you eat eggs, and a supplement-dose caution for anyone considering one beyond a standard multivitamin.',
   'If you follow a vegetarian or vegan diet: soy foods, cruciferous vegetables and legumes contribute choline, though in smaller amounts than eggs, meat or fish give — regular, varied inclusion across your week matters here. If you''re considering a choline supplement: check the dose with your own doctor first — very high intakes, mainly from concentrated supplements rather than food, have been linked to a fishy body odour, a drop in blood pressure and, at very high doses, liver toxicity. If you have a specific lipid or heart condition your doctor is monitoring: ask your own doctor about egg yolk and other dietary cholesterol sources specifically, since individual guidance can differ from general population advice. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content — your own prescribed meal plan or hypoglycaemia guidance takes precedence over these general suggestions. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, and are also dependable choline sources. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and choline content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Hyderabadi cooking (khatti dal, dalcha, bagara baingan, mirchi ka salan, khameeri roti, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Choline supports your liver function, the structure of your cell membranes, and your nervous system. Egg yolk, meat, fish and dairy are the richest everyday sources; soy foods, legumes and cruciferous vegetables contribute smaller but genuine amounts. It''s a nutrient that rarely gets discussed, which is partly why many diets run a little light on it without anyone noticing.", "breakfast_a": "Khatti dal poori (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Khagina, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khatti dal, sour lentil curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bagara baingan", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Hyderabadi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bagara baingan", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "If your diet is vegetarian or vegan", "notes": "Soy foods, cruciferous vegetables and legumes all contribute choline, though in smaller amounts than eggs, meat or fish give. Regular, varied inclusion of these across your week matters more here than for many other nutrients this series has covered — there isn''t one standout plant source that covers the gap on its own.", "breakfast_a": "Khameeri roti (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalcha, lentil and bottle gourd curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mirchi ka salan", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Hyderabadi-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mirchi ka salan", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a chikoo", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Soya chunk curry, measured 1/2 cup cooked soya chunks, with roti (2 pieces)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Egg yolk is one of the most concentrated sources", "notes": "Egg yolk carries most of an egg''s choline. Eggs are one useful choline source; include them according to your own preferences and care plan, rather than aiming for a set number most days. General concerns about dietary cholesterol from eggs have been reassessed for most people in recent years, but if you have a specific lipid or heart condition your doctor is monitoring, ask what''s right for you specifically rather than assuming general advice applies.", "breakfast_a": "Bagara khichdi (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khatti dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tamatar ka salan", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Hyderabadi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tamatar ka salan", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut pineapple", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you take a high-dose choline supplement", "notes": "Very high choline intakes — mainly from concentrated supplements rather than food — have been linked to a fishy body odour, a drop in blood pressure, and, at very high doses, liver toxicity — not just minor discomfort. Ordinary food sources don''t carry this risk. If you''re considering a dedicated choline supplement beyond a standard multivitamin, check the dose with your own doctor first.", "breakfast_a": "Khameeri roti (2 pieces) with a khatti dal and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Toor dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bagara baingan", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Hyderabadi-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bagara baingan", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Cruciferous vegetables contribute too", "notes": "Broccoli, cauliflower and cabbage all contribute modest but real amounts of choline alongside their other nutrients — not a replacement for eggs, meat or soy if you eat those, but a genuine part of the picture if you don''t, or simply a reason to keep including them regularly either way.", "breakfast_a": "Khameeri roti (2 pieces) with a cabbage sabzi and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khatti dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower curry", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Hyderabadi-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a matar dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-spinach curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one dish", "notes": "As with the rest of this series, no single meal needs to carry your choline for the day — eggs, meat, fish, dairy, soy, legumes or cruciferous vegetables, whichever you eat, showing up regularly across your week is what actually adds up.", "breakfast_a": "Sabudana khichdi (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-coriander sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Toor dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a broccoli sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Hyderabadi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a broccoli sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a kiwi", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a chikki square, jaggery-almond (1 piece)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Before this week, had choline ever come up on your radar at all — and does anything here change how you''ll eat eggs, soy or cruciferous vegetables going forward?", "breakfast_a": "Khatti dal poori (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Khagina, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khatti dal, sour lentil curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bagara baingan", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Hyderabadi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bagara baingan", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is there a nutrient in this series that still feels like a mystery to you, worth reading up on a little more in your own time?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another everyday theme — still grounded in the same idea: your own nourishment deserves the same attention as everyone else''s at the table.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 106: Your own teeth and gums, and what your diet has to do with them (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   106,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Your own teeth and gums, and what your diet has to do with them',
   '"Pregnancy and breastfeeding place real, if often invisible, demands on your calcium and mineral stores — and how often you snack matters for cavity risk as much as what you snack on. Your teeth deserve the same attention you give everyone else''s."',
   'Pregnancy and breastfeeding place real demands on your bone mineral stores — calcium, vitamin D and phosphorus all matter here. Your teeth are a somewhat separate story: enamel doesn''t remodel or release calcium the way bone does, so despite the old idea that pregnancy costs you "a tooth for every child," it isn''t drawing mineral directly out of your teeth. What does shape your own cavity risk is dietary and behavioural — how often you eat across the day matters for that risk as much as what you eat. Brushing twice daily with fluoride toothpaste and cleaning between your teeth daily remain the everyday basics that matter most here — rinsing with water after a snack is a helpful extra, not a substitute for these. This week also looks at snacking frequency versus quantity, vitamin C''s role in gum health, and two things worth flagging to a dentist rather than assuming they''ll pass on their own: persistent dry mouth or new tooth sensitivity, and pregnancy-related gum changes.',
   'If you''re pregnant again: gum swelling or bleeding can be a hormonal, pregnancy-related change — a dental check-up remains safe and recommended during pregnancy. If you''ve noticed persistent dry mouth or new tooth sensitivity: mention it at a dental check-up rather than waiting it out — there are several possible causes worth checking, not only a postpartum one. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: gum disease risk can be higher, so keep to your usual dental check-up schedule and mention your condition to your dentist; also note the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content — your own prescribed meal plan or hypoglycaemia guidance takes precedence. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team, and from a dentist for anything dental, always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a mixed rice dish is a heaped cup (about 250g total); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a phulka or roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein and mineral content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Sindhi cooking (sai bhaji, koki, dal pakwan, seyal, bhugha chawar, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Calcium, vitamin D and phosphorus support your bone health through pregnancy and breastfeeding, and matter for your teeth too — though teeth work differently from bone. Enamel doesn''t remodel or release calcium the way bone does, so despite the old idea of ''losing a tooth for every child,'' pregnancy and breastfeeding aren''t drawing mineral directly out of your teeth. What actually shapes your own cavity risk day to day is dietary: how often you snack across the day matters as much as what the snack contains — frequent grazing gives cavity-causing bacteria more opportunities to produce acid than the same food eaten in one sitting.", "breakfast_a": "Koki (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Sindhi omelette, onion-tomato (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Sai bhaji (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Sindhi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sai bhaji side", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Phulka (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Seyal chicken (100g chicken) with phulka (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Frequency matters as much as quantity", "notes": "Sipping a sweet drink or grazing on something starchy across several hours gives bacteria in your mouth more opportunities to produce the acid that wears at enamel than the same amount eaten at one sitting. This isn''t about cutting foods out — it''s about noticing whether snacking has become continuous rather than occasional, and rinsing with water afterward when you can.", "breakfast_a": "Dal pakwan (1 serving) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kadhi (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Sindhi-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kadhi side", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Phulka (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with phulka (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "Curd with fruit, or another helping of your meal — drink water first if you''re thirsty too"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Vitamin C supports your gums too", "notes": "Vitamin C plays a role in gum tissue health, not only in healing or immunity — citrus, amla, guava, capsicum and tomatoes all contribute. Gums that bleed easily when you brush are worth mentioning to a dentist rather than assuming it''s simply from brushing too hard.", "breakfast_a": "Besan aur pyaz paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Sai bhaji (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tomato-onion salad", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Sindhi-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tomato-onion salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Phulka (2 pieces) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with phulka (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you''ve noticed dry mouth or new tooth sensitivity", "notes": "Dry mouth or new tooth sensitivity can have several causes. A dental assessment can help identify the cause rather than assuming it is related to childbirth. Saliva helps neutralise acid and protect enamel, so a persistently dry mouth can raise cavity risk whatever the cause. Staying hydrated, chewing sugar-free gum if that suits you, and mentioning it at a dental check-up are all reasonable steps.", "breakfast_a": "Sindhi kadhi with rice, small portion (1 cup rice, 1/2 cup kadhi) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Bhugha chawar (1 cup) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Sindhi-style (100g chicken) with bhugha chawar (1 cup)", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Phulka (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with phulka (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "Curd with fruit, or another helping of your meal — drink water first if you''re thirsty too"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If you''re pregnant again, or your teeth or gums have felt different lately", "notes": "Hormonal shifts in pregnancy can make gums more prone to swelling and bleeding, sometimes called pregnancy gingivitis — good oral hygiene and a dental check-up remain safe and recommended during pregnancy despite some lingering myths otherwise. If you notice looser teeth, persistent gum pain or bleeding beyond routine brushing, see a dentist rather than waiting.", "breakfast_a": "Loli, Sindhi jaggery flatbread (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Sai bhaji (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a measured 1/2 cup cooked masoor dal", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Sindhi-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sai bhaji side", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Phulka (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with phulka (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one meal", "notes": "A dependable calcium source at more than one meal, vitamin C across your week, water as your default drink between meals, and a dental check-up if it''s been a while — none of this needs to be perfect, and none of it needs to happen today. It adds up the same way the rest of this series has: gradually, across ordinary days.", "breakfast_a": "Seyal roti with tamatar (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-coriander sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Sai bhaji (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Sindhi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kadhi side", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a kiwi", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a chikki square, jaggery-sesame (1 piece)", "dinner_a": "Phulka (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with phulka (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. When did you last have a dental check-up — and is this a week you could book one, even a routine cleaning?", "breakfast_a": "Koki (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Sindhi omelette, onion-tomato (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Sai bhaji (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Sindhi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sai bhaji side", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Phulka (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Seyal chicken (100g chicken) with phulka (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Has anything about your own body — teeth included — felt overlooked since your focus shifted to your child? What would it take to give it a little attention this month?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another everyday theme — still grounded in the same idea: your own nourishment deserves the same attention as everyone else''s at the table.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 107: Noticing your own relationship with food, without judgment (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   107,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Noticing your own relationship with food, without judgment',
   '"Reaching for something comforting after a hard day is human, not a failure — and so is a plate that doesn''t get finished. This week is about noticing your own patterns around food with a little curiosity, not correcting them."',
   'Many of the habits we carry around food — finishing a plate out of habit, reaching for something comforting after a hard day, feeling that a treat needs to be earned — were often learned long before motherhood. This week isn''t about changing any of them. It''s simply an invitation to notice your own patterns with a little curiosity and without judgment, the same way this series has asked you to notice your appetite, your energy and your needs all along. Nothing here labels any food as off-limits, and nothing asks you to track, limit or earn what you eat. If food has become one of very few ways you have to cope, that''s worth a conversation with your doctor — not because any single meal is a problem, but because you deserve more than one kind of support.',
   'If eating has started to feel distressing, or food feels like it''s taking up more mental space than it used to: please talk to your doctor. That''s a conversation worth having on its own, separate from anything in this series. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content — your own prescribed meal plan or hypoglycaemia guidance takes precedence over these general suggestions, including around treats and portion timing. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite, and stopping before a plate is finished is just as fine as finishing it. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a mixed rice dish is a heaped cup (about 250g total); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti or litti is 2 pieces; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Bihari cooking (litti chokha, sattu paratha, chana ghugni, dal pitha, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Many of us carry habits around food from long before motherhood — finishing a plate because leaving food felt wasteful or ungrateful, or reaching for something sweet or fried after a hard day because it genuinely helps in the moment. Neither is a flaw to fix this week. The aim here is simply noticing the pattern, without deciding it needs to change.", "breakfast_a": "Sattu paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana ghugni (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a baingan chokha", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Bihari-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a baingan chokha", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut mango", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Litti (2 pieces) with chokha and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "A treat isn''t something to earn", "notes": "Framing dessert or a favourite snack as something you''ve \"earned\" through a hard workout or a difficult day can quietly turn ordinary food into a reward system with rules attached. A sweet or a fried snack can simply be a food you enjoy sometimes, with no ledger attached.", "breakfast_a": "Poori-aloo sabzi (2 pooris) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dal pitha (4 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a baingan chokha", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Bihari-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an aloo chokha", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "If food is one of the ways you cope with a hard day", "notes": "Using food for comfort now and then is ordinary and not something to feel guilty about. If it''s become one of the only ways you have to unwind, or leaves you feeling worse afterward more often than not, that''s worth mentioning to your doctor — not as a diet problem, but as a sign it may help to have other kinds of support in place too, alongside food, not instead of it.", "breakfast_a": "Chana dal poori (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana ghugni (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tomato-onion salad", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Bihari-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tomato-onion salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "A plate that isn''t finished is fine too", "notes": "The instinct to finish everything on your plate — often learned early, sometimes tied to not wanting to waste food — can override your own fullness cues without you noticing. Stopping when you''re satisfied, even with food left over, isn''t wasteful; a container in the fridge is one option, and so is simply leaving it.", "breakfast_a": "Khichdi (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dal pitha (4 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a saag chokha", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Bihari-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a saag chokha", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Enjoying a treat and eating well aren''t opposites", "notes": "A sweet, a fried snack or a favourite dessert doesn''t cancel out the rest of a generally varied week, and it doesn''t need to be balanced out by skipping a later meal. Food doesn''t have to be earned or repaid — this week''s meals include room for whatever you''d genuinely enjoy, alongside everything else.", "breakfast_a": "Chana daal cheela (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dal pitha (4 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a tomato chokha", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Bihari-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tomato chokha", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a thekua (1 piece)", "dinner_a": "Litti (2 pieces) with chokha and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one meal", "notes": "One meal you didn''t finish, one evening you reached for something comforting, one dessert enjoyed without a second thought — none of it needs re-examining. What matters, as with everything else in this series, is the pattern across your whole week, met with the same kindness you''d offer anyone else.", "breakfast_a": "Aloo paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-coriander sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dal pitha (4 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a methi chokha", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Bihari-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a methi chokha", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a kiwi", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Did you notice any of your own patterns this week — a treat you didn''t need to feel guilty about, a plate you left unfinished without a second thought? No answer here is a wrong one.", "breakfast_a": "Sattu paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana ghugni (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a baingan chokha", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Bihari-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a baingan chokha", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut mango", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Litti (2 pieces) with chokha and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is there a food rule you''ve been carrying since childhood that, looking at it now, doesn''t need to apply to you anymore?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another everyday theme — still grounded in the same idea: your own nourishment deserves the same attention as everyone else''s at the table.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 108: Your everyday drinks and nourishment (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   108,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Your everyday drinks and nourishment',
   '"Juice, flavoured milk and malt ''health drink'' powders are worth an honest look in your own everyday routine. None of them are forbidden; knowing what''s actually in the glass just helps you choose on purpose."',
   'Juice, flavoured milk and malt "health drink" powders are worth a closer, honest look in your own everyday routine. None of these drinks are forbidden here; the point is simply knowing what''s actually in the glass, so any sweetened drink you enjoy is a choice rather than a habit you didn''t examine. This week also covers what breastfeeding actually asks of your fluid intake — water, mostly — and how sports drinks, energy drinks and medical oral rehydration solutions differ, since they''re often treated as interchangeable when they aren''t.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on juice, sweetened drinks and portion timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you''re pregnant again: keep caffeine from tea, coffee and energy drinks combined under 200 mg a day, and avoid energy drinks specifically — check labels, since caffeine content varies widely. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); pakhala is a cup of cooked rice (about 150g), measured separately from the water, cooled boiled water, or buttermilk it''s soaked in; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Odia cooking (dalma, santula, chakuli pitha, pakhala, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Fruit juice, even 100% juice, delivers sugar without the fibre whole fruit gives, and it''s easy to drink far more of it than you''d eat as whole fruit. Whole fruit remains the better everyday default; juice occasionally, in a modest glass, is a reasonable choice rather than an off-limits one. Worth knowing: For pakhala, use freshly cooked rice that''s been cooled and refrigerated promptly, then soaked in cooled boiled water or buttermilk just before eating — this is simpler and safer than leaving rice to ferment at room temperature overnight.", "breakfast_a": "Chakuli pitha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalma (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a santula", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Odia-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a santula", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Pakhala (1 cup cooked rice, soaked separately) with a small bowl of curd (150g), a saga bhaja, and a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal of your choice", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Malt ''health drink'' powders — check the label, formulations vary", "notes": "Malt-based drink powders vary in ingredients, added sugar and fortification. Compare the label for serving size, added sugar and nutrients. Milk itself contributes protein and calcium; the powder''s contribution varies by product.", "breakfast_a": "Poda pitha (1 slice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalma (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhaja", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Odia-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhaja", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a dalma (3/4 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Sugared tea and coffee add up quietly", "notes": "Two or three cups of sugared tea or coffee across a day can add a surprising amount of sugar without registering as a \"sweet drink\" at all. Cutting back gradually, or getting used to less sugar over a couple of weeks, tends to work better than stopping all at once.", "breakfast_a": "Enduri pitha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalma (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a saga bhaja", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Odia-style (100g fish, machha jhola) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a saga bhaja", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you''re breastfeeding, water is still your main need", "notes": "Breastfeeding increases your fluid needs, but water remains the most dependable way to meet them — sweetened drinks aren''t required to \"keep supply up,\" despite that idea circulating widely. Keeping a water bottle within reach during feeds is a simple habit that covers the real need directly.", "breakfast_a": "Manda pitha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalma (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a besara", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Odia-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a besara", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "Curd with fruit, or another helping of your meal — drink water first if you''re thirsty too"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Sports drinks, energy drinks and rehydration solutions aren''t the same thing", "notes": "Water usually suits ordinary activity. Sports drinks may have a role during prolonged, strenuous exercise, but they aren''t formulated for the same purpose as energy drinks, which aren''t a hydration requirement at all. Neither one replaces an appropriate oral rehydration solution (ORS) when one is medically needed — ask your pharmacist or doctor for that specifically rather than substituting a sports or energy drink (CDC rehydration guidance).", "breakfast_a": "Chuda ghasa, spiced flattened rice (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalma (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a potato besara", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Odia-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a potato besara", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Pakhala (1 cup cooked rice, soaked separately) with a small bowl of curd (150g), a bhaja, and a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal of your choice", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one glass", "notes": "One glass of juice, one cup of sweet tea, one malt drink enjoyed for the taste — none of it needs re-examining on its own. Water as your everyday default, with sweetened drinks as an occasional choice rather than a daily habit, is what actually shapes the pattern.", "breakfast_a": "Sag pitha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-coriander sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalma (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi bhaja", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Odia-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi bhaja", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a kiwi", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a chhena poda bite (1 small piece)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. What does a typical day of drinks actually look like for you — and is there one swap here, water for one sweetened drink a day, that would feel easy rather than restrictive?", "breakfast_a": "Chakuli pitha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalma (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a santula", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Odia-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a santula", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Pakhala (1 cup cooked rice, soaked separately) with a small bowl of curd (150g), a saga bhaja, and a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal of your choice", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Looking back over this whole run of weeks, what''s one change that''s actually stuck — and what''s one thing you''re glad you didn''t feel pressured to change?',
   'This closes the current run of weekly themes. Whatever comes next in this series, the idea underneath all of it stays the same: your own nourishment deserves the same attention as everyone else''s at the table.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 109: Magnesium for muscle, nerve and bone health (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   109,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Magnesium for muscle, nerve and bone health',
   '"Magnesium works quietly in the background — muscle and nerve function, energy metabolism, bone health alongside calcium — and a diet leaning on refined grains and packaged food can run short on it without any single dramatic sign."',
   'Magnesium supports hundreds of processes in your body, including muscle and nerve function, energy metabolism, and bone health alongside calcium. It rarely gets much attention because a shortfall doesn''t usually produce one obvious symptom — more often it shows up as general fatigue, muscle cramps, or nothing noticeable at all. Breastfeeding doesn''t meaningfully raise your magnesium requirement above the standard adult recommendation, but many everyday diets built around refined grains and packaged food fall short of it regardless. Whole grains, legumes, nuts, seeds and leafy greens are the main food sources — building meals around these, rather than reaching for a supplement first, is the more dependable starting point.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you have kidney disease: follow your renal team''s advice on food choices and supplements. Check before using magnesium supplements or magnesium-containing antacids or laxatives. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. This week''s meals also lean on whole grains, legumes, nuts and seeds partly for their magnesium contribution — keep including these regularly rather than treating any single day''s total as a target to hit. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Parsi cooking (dhansak, akuri, patra ni machhi, sali boti, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Magnesium supports muscle and nerve function, steady energy metabolism, and bone health alongside calcium. Whole grains, legumes, nuts, seeds and leafy greens are the main everyday sources — a diet built mostly around refined grains and packaged food can quietly run short.", "breakfast_a": "Vaghareli khichdi, tempered rice-and-lentil khichdi (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Akuri, Parsi-style spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vegetable dhansak (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked mixed lentils) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a kachumber salad", "lunch_b": "Mutton dhansak (100g mutton, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked mixed lentils) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a kachumber salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Masoor usli, Parsi-style lentil dish (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces)", "dinner_b": "Sali boti, mutton in a tomato gravy (100g mutton) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Magnesium and sleep — a two-way relationship", "notes": "Magnesium is involved in the pathways that help regulate sleep, and poor sleep can in turn affect how your body handles food and appetite the next day. Evidence for magnesium supplements specifically improving sleep is limited and mixed, so getting it from food alongside ordinary sleep habits is the more dependable approach than expecting a supplement to fix a sleep problem on its own.", "breakfast_a": "Bhaji pav, spiced mixed vegetable curry with pav (1 cup bhaji, 2 pav) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala French toast, egg-coated bread (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vegetable dhansak (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked mixed lentils) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhinda nu shaak", "lunch_b": "Chicken dhansak (100g chicken, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked mixed lentils) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhinda nu shaak", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Muscle cramps and magnesium — what the evidence actually shows", "notes": "Magnesium is often suggested as a fix for muscle cramps. The evidence that magnesium supplements specifically prevent cramps is mixed and not strong enough to promise relief — adequate magnesium from food remains worthwhile for its other roles, but isn''t a guaranteed cramp cure. Persistent or severe cramping is still worth mentioning to your doctor.", "breakfast_a": "Ravo, savoury semolina upma-style (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Sali per eedu, eggs baked over crisp potato straws (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vaal ni dal, Parsi-style bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked vaal beans) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a sali nu shaak", "lunch_b": "Patra ni machhi, fish steamed in a banana leaf (100g fish) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a sali nu shaak", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you take a magnesium supplement", "notes": "Magnesium supplements can cause loose stools or stomach upset at higher doses, and how much your kidneys can safely process depends on how well they''re working. If you''re considering a supplement, check with your doctor or pharmacist first, especially if you take other medication or have kidney disease — magnesium-containing antacids and laxatives carry the same consideration.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable sev, tempered vermicelli with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with pav (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vegetable dhansak (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked mixed lentils) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a bharwa marcha, stuffed pepper", "lunch_b": "Mutton dhansak (100g mutton, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked mixed lentils) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a bharwa marcha, stuffed pepper", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Whole grains and legumes as your everyday magnesium source", "notes": "Whole grains (like brown rice and whole wheat) and legumes (lentils, beans, chana) are dependable everyday magnesium sources, alongside nuts, seeds and leafy greens — no single food needs to carry the whole day, and swapping some refined grain for a whole-grain equivalent across the week adds up.", "breakfast_a": "Handvo, baked lentil-and-rice cake (1 slice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Kheema pav, spiced minced mutton with pav (75g mutton, 2 pav) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vaghareli dal, tempered plain lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a papeta nu shaak, potato curry", "lunch_b": "Farcha, Parsi-style fried chicken (100g chicken) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a papeta nu shaak, potato curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one food", "notes": "No single dal, grain or handful of nuts needs to be perfect. Magnesium adds up across a week of ordinary meals built around whole grains, legumes, nuts, seeds and leafy greens — that pattern is what actually counts, not any one day''s total.", "breakfast_a": "Masala poha with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vegetable dhansak (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked mixed lentils) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a vegetable sali", "lunch_b": "Kolmi no patio, prawns in a sweet-sour curry (100g prawns) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a vegetable sali", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a bhinda nu shaak", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Which of this week''s whole-grain or legume dishes could become a regular fixture in your own week, not just this one?", "breakfast_a": "Vaghareli khichdi, tempered rice-and-lentil khichdi (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Akuri, Parsi-style spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vegetable dhansak (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked mixed lentils) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a kachumber salad", "lunch_b": "Mutton dhansak (100g mutton, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked mixed lentils) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a kachumber salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Masoor usli, Parsi-style lentil dish (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces)", "dinner_b": "Sali boti, mutton in a tomato gravy (100g mutton) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'Next week continues this run of weekly themes, each focused on a different piece of your own everyday nourishment.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 110: How your own sleep affects your appetite and eating (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   110,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How your own sleep affects your appetite and eating',
   '"Poor sleep can affect hunger, food preferences and eating patterns, though the exact mechanism varies from person to person. Noticing that pattern is more useful than fighting it with willpower alone."',
   'Sleep and eating are closely linked, and that link runs in both directions. When sleep is short or repeatedly interrupted — common at any stage of parenting, not only in the earliest months — appetite, food preferences and eating patterns can all be affected, even though the exact mechanism varies from person to person. This week isn''t about fixing your sleep; it''s about noticing how sleep affects your eating so a poor-sleep day''s food choices feel less like a mystery. Regular meals, a protein-forward breakfast, and mindful caffeine timing are the practical, food-focused pieces that are actually within reach on a tiring day.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If sleep loss has you relying heavily on caffeine: keep total intake under 200mg a day while breastfeeding, and check labels on any energy drink or extra-strength coffee — caffeine content varies more than people expect. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite, especially on a short-sleep day when your appetite may genuinely be higher. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Chhattisgarhi cooking (chila, faraa, aamat, muthia, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Short or interrupted sleep can affect appetite, food preferences and eating patterns. Some studies find changes in hunger-related hormones, but the response varies from person to person. Feeling hungrier after poor sleep is not a willpower failure — it''s worth understanding rather than fighting.", "breakfast_a": "Chila, fermented rice-and-lentil crepe (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Aamat, mixed vegetable curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal or beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhajia", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Chhattisgarhi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhajia", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What the research on sleep and hunger hormones actually shows", "notes": "Some studies find that short sleep raises ghrelin (a hunger-signalling hormone) and lowers leptin (a fullness-signalling hormone), but not every study finds this pattern, and people who eat more after poor sleep don''t always show these hormone changes. The safer takeaway is that poor sleep can affect hunger and eating in ways that vary by person, not through one single predictable mechanism.", "breakfast_a": "Faraa, steamed rice-and-lentil dumplings (4 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dubki kadhi, gram-flour-and-curd curry (3/4 cup serving, made with a measured 20g dry besan and 150g curd per serving) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a saag", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Chhattisgarhi-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a saag", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Protein at breakfast, especially after bad sleep", "notes": "A protein-rich breakfast tends to support steadier fullness through a tiring morning better than a carbohydrate-heavy one alone. It''s especially worth leaning on this specifically after a rough night, without needing to change your usual breakfast every other day.", "breakfast_a": "Chousela roti with a chana ghugni (2 pieces, 1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Aamat, mixed vegetable curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal or beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a chana ghugni", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, river-fish style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a chana ghugni", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Caffeine can help, but it can also backfire", "notes": "Caffeine can genuinely help you function after poor sleep, but relying on it late in the day can make the next night''s sleep worse, continuing the cycle. Keep total caffeine under 200mg a day while breastfeeding, and try to front-load it earlier rather than reaching for more in the evening.", "breakfast_a": "Angakar roti with a dubki kadhi (2 pieces, 1 cup kadhi) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled-egg curry (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Aamat, mixed vegetable curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal or beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi bhaja", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Chhattisgarhi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi bhaja", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a bhajia", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Regular meals matter more when sleep is irregular", "notes": "Skipping meals when you''re exhausted often backfires, leading to bigger swings in hunger and energy later in the day. Keeping roughly regular meal times, even on a rough-sleep day, tends to help more than trying to power through on caffeine alone.", "breakfast_a": "Muthia, steamed-and-tempered lentil dumplings (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Muthia curry, steamed lentil dumplings in curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a potato curry", "lunch_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a potato curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one bad night", "notes": "No single bad night needs fixing with a perfect diet the next day. The pattern across a week — regular meals, protein at breakfast, caffeine that doesn''t creep too late — matters more than any one day''s response to a rough night.", "breakfast_a": "Chhattisgarhi-style vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Aamat, mixed vegetable curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal or beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki curry", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Chhattisgarhi-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a saag", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Can you spot a pattern from this past week between a poor night''s sleep and how the next day''s eating actually went?", "breakfast_a": "Chila, fermented rice-and-lentil crepe (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Aamat, mixed vegetable curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal or beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhajia", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Chhattisgarhi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhajia", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'Next week continues this run of weekly themes, each focused on a different piece of your own everyday nourishment.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 111: Should you take a probiotic? Curd, fermented foods and your own gut health (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   111,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Should you take a probiotic? Curd, fermented foods and your own gut health',
   '"A daily probiotic supplement hasn''t been shown necessary for the routine wellness of an otherwise healthy adult — and that''s reassuring, not a gap to fill. The fermented foods already in your kitchen carry plenty of value on their own."',
   'Probiotic gummies, drops and powders are widely marketed with confident claims about immunity, digestion or mood. Probiotics are live microorganisms shown, in a specific dose and strain, to provide a proven health benefit — a narrower definition than many products on the shelf imply. Routine daily probiotic supplementation has not been established as necessary for general health, immunity, mood or digestion in someone who isn''t sick or on antibiotics; the evidence behind most marketed claims is thin, mixed, or simply hasn''t been well studied. Fermented foods already common in many Indian kitchens — curd, idli and dosa among them — are valuable in their own right for taste, digestibility and nutrition, without needing to meet that stricter "proven probiotic" bar. For most healthy adults, commonly available probiotic products appear low-risk, but low-risk doesn''t mean universally necessary or appropriate — effects and safety depend on the organism, product quality, dose and your own health.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you are immunocompromised, critically ill, or have a central venous catheter: check with your care team before taking a probiotic supplement — rare but serious infections have been reported in these specific groups. Follow your care team''s food-safety advice for fermented and live-culture foods too; do not assume every product or home preparation is suitable. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Uttarakhandi / Kumaoni-Garhwali cooking (jholi, bhatt ki churkani, kafuli, mandua roti, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "A daily probiotic supplement hasn''t been shown necessary for the routine wellness of an otherwise healthy adult. Probiotics are live microorganisms shown, in a specific dose and strain, to provide a proven health benefit — a narrower definition than many marketed products imply. Routine probiotic supplementation has not been established as necessary for general wellness in someone who isn''t sick or on antibiotics.", "breakfast_a": "Mandua roti, finger millet flatbread (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Jholi, Kumaoni curd-and-gram-flour curry (1 cup, made with a measured 200g curd and 2 tablespoons besan) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kafuli", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Pahadi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kafuli", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What actually counts as a \"live culture\" food", "notes": "Curd or yogurt may contain live cultures when it hasn''t been heat-treated after fermentation — but idli, dosa and similar foods are made through fermentation, then cooked, so the finished food shouldn''t be described as a source of live bacteria. Both remain valuable for taste, texture and nutrition regardless of that distinction.", "breakfast_a": "Jhangora kheer, barnyard millet pudding (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Bhatt ki churkani, black soybean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked black soybeans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kafuli", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Pahadi-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kafuli", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a gahat dal, horse-gram dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Antibiotics and probiotics — a narrower, evidence-backed use", "notes": "Some specific probiotic strains show possible benefit for antibiotic-associated diarrhoea, but that finding doesn''t transfer to every strain, product or situation. If you''re prescribed antibiotics, ask your doctor or pharmacist whether a specific probiotic is worth adding, rather than assuming any product on the shelf will help.", "breakfast_a": "Swaale, sweet fried bread (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gahat dal, horse-gram dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked horse gram) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an aloo ke gutke", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, hill-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an aloo ke gutke", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you''re considering a probiotic supplement", "notes": "If you''re considering a probiotic supplement for a specific reason, check the strain and dose against what''s actually been studied for that use — many products list only a genus and species without the specific strain that clinical evidence exists for, and that detail matters more than a general claim on the label.", "breakfast_a": "Gahat ke dubke, horse-gram dumplings (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Jholi, Kumaoni curd-and-gram-flour curry (1 cup, made with a measured 200g curd and 2 tablespoons besan) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an aloo ke gutke", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Pahadi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an aloo ke gutke", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a kafuli", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Fermented foods already in your kitchen", "notes": "Curd, idli, dosa and other fermented foods are worth keeping regardless of the probiotic question — they''re valuable for taste, digestibility and nutrition in their own right, without needing to meet a stricter clinical bar to earn a place on your plate.", "breakfast_a": "Bhatt ki churkani, black soybean curry with mandua roti (1 cup churkani, 2 roti) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Jholi, Kumaoni curd-and-gram-flour curry (1 cup, made with a measured 200g curd and 2 tablespoons besan) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a rayo ka saag", "lunch_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a rayo ka saag", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one supplement", "notes": "No single fermented food or supplement decision needs to carry your gut health. Regular meals with fibre, varied vegetables and the fermented foods already familiar to you matter more, day to day, than any one product.", "breakfast_a": "Aloo ke gutke with puri (1 cup gutke, 2 puri) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Jholi, Kumaoni curd-and-gram-flour curry (1 cup, made with a measured 200g curd and 2 tablespoons besan) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki curry", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Pahadi-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a bhatt ki churkani", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Do you already eat fermented foods most days without thinking of them as anything special — and does that change how you feel about the probiotic-aisle claims?", "breakfast_a": "Mandua roti, finger millet flatbread (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Jholi, Kumaoni curd-and-gram-flour curry (1 cup, made with a measured 200g curd and 2 tablespoons besan) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kafuli", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Pahadi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kafuli", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'Next week continues this run of weekly themes, each focused on a different piece of your own everyday nourishment.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 112: Ultra-processed foods in your own everyday diet — what "processed" actually means (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   112,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Ultra-processed foods in your own everyday diet — what "processed" actually means',
   '""Processed" isn''t a single category — a frozen dal or tinned tomato is processed too, just not the way a packaged snack is. Knowing the difference matters more than trying to eliminate processing altogether."',
   '"Processed food" gets used as a catch-all, but it covers everything from washed and bagged spinach to a flavoured instant snack — very different things nutritionally. Researchers increasingly use a classification (NOVA) that separates unprocessed and minimally processed foods, processed culinary ingredients, processed foods, and ultra-processed foods specifically — industrially formulated products built from additives and refined ingredients rarely found in a home kitchen. Observational studies link a diet high in ultra-processed food to a range of health outcomes, though this remains an active, still-developing area of research rather than settled, proven causation — and it isn''t a reason to treat any single packaged food as forbidden. Knowing which category a food actually falls into is more useful than trying to eliminate "processed food" altogether, which isn''t realistic or necessary.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Coorg / Kodava cooking (kadambuttu, akki roti, koli curry, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Nutrition researchers use a classification called NOVA to group foods by how processed they are: unprocessed or minimally processed foods (vegetables, grains, curd, eggs); processed culinary ingredients (oil, ghee, sugar, salt); processed foods (canned vegetables, cheese, freshly made bread); and ultra-processed foods (industrially formulated products with additives rarely found in a home kitchen — packaged snacks, instant noodles, sugary drinks, reconstituted meat products). This week focuses on that last category specifically. Worth knowing: Use commercially prepared bamboo shoots and follow the package instructions. Do not use raw or inadequately prepared fresh shoots.", "breakfast_a": "Akki roti, rice flatbread (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Coorg-style vegetable-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bamboo shoot curry", "lunch_b": "Koli curry, Coorg-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bamboo shoot curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Reading an ingredients list, not just the front of the pack", "notes": "Checking an ingredients list, not just front-of-pack claims like \"natural\" or \"no added sugar,\" is a more reliable way to spot ultra-processed food. A long list including things you wouldn''t keep in your own kitchen — emulsifiers, flavour enhancers, artificial colours, several types of added sugar under different names — is a useful clue. That said, a long or unfamiliar-sounding ingredients list doesn''t by itself make a food good or bad for you nutritionally — it''s one clue among several, not a verdict on its own.", "breakfast_a": "Kadambuttu, steamed rice dumplings (3 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Thovve, Coorg-style dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kumm curry, mushroom curry", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Coorg-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kumm curry, mushroom curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Convenience foods aren''t automatically a problem — context matters", "notes": "Convenience foods can support affordable, practical nourishment. Processing categories depend on ingredients and preparation: bread made from flour, water, yeast and salt is processed, while some formulations are ultra-processed. Some ultra-processed foods can still contribute useful fibre, protein or other nutrients — the category label alone doesn''t settle whether a food is worth including.", "breakfast_a": "Noolputtu, string hoppers (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Coorg-style vegetable-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans thoran", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Kodava-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans thoran", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Sugary drinks and packaged snacks — a practical place to start", "notes": "Reducing how often you have sugary drinks or low-fibre packaged snacks is a practical, worthwhile place to start if you''re looking to make one change — both tend to be high in added sugar or refined starch and low in fibre. This isn''t a claim that these are scientifically proven to matter more than every other ultra-processed category, just a realistic place to begin.", "breakfast_a": "Thaliya puttu, rice-and-coconut steamed cake (1 piece) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Coorg-style vegetable-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage curry", "lunch_b": "Koli curry, Coorg-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage curry", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Small, sustainable swaps rather than an all-or-nothing overhaul", "notes": "Small, sustainable swaps — a packaged snack traded for fruit and nuts a few times a week, water instead of a sugary drink some days — tend to stick better than an all-or-nothing overhaul, and they don''t require treating every packaged food as off-limits.", "breakfast_a": "Akki roti with a chutney (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Beans-and-lentil curry, Coorg-style (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a raw banana curry", "lunch_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a raw banana curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not any single food", "notes": "No single ultra-processed snack or meal needs to be treated as a problem on its own. The overall pattern across your week — how much of your regular eating leans on ultra-processed food versus everything else — is what the evidence actually speaks to, not any individual choice.", "breakfast_a": "Kadambuttu with a kumm curry (3 pieces, 1/2 cup curry) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Coorg-style vegetable-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin curry", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Coorg-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a bamboo shoot curry", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Without judging it either way, what''s one ultra-processed food that shows up most often in your own week — and is a small swap for it something you''d actually want to try?", "breakfast_a": "Akki roti, rice flatbread (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Coorg-style vegetable-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bamboo shoot curry", "lunch_b": "Koli curry, Coorg-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bamboo shoot curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'Next week continues this run of weekly themes, each focused on a different piece of your own everyday nourishment.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 113: Eating without distraction — noticing your own meals again (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   113,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating without distraction — noticing your own meals again',
   '"A meal eaten in front of a screen or while multitasking can affect how aware you are of it and how much you eat — but your meal''s size and composition still matter too, not just how present you were for it."',
   'Eating while distracted — scrolling a phone, watching something, working through a meal — is linked in research to reduced awareness and memory of what you ate, and may influence how much some people eat. This doesn''t mean your meal''s size, composition or your actual hunger don''t matter too — if you''re hungry after eating, it''s fine to eat more, distracted or not. This week is about attention during eating itself, distinct from your broader relationship with food. This isn''t about achieving a quiet, sit-down meal every time — that''s rarely realistic with a young child around, and it doesn''t need to be the goal. Smaller, on-the-go, or reheated meals are just as real and nourishing as a fully plated one; the aim here is simply noticing your food a little more often than not, in whatever form the meal takes.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Jharkhandi cooking (dhuska, pittha, chilka roti, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Distraction can affect awareness and memory of a meal, and may influence how much some people eat. Your meal''s size and composition, activity and other needs still matter too — if you''re hungry afterwards, it''s okay to eat.", "breakfast_a": "Dhuska, fried rice-and-lentil pancake (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Jharkhandi-style toor dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a koinar saag", "lunch_b": "Desi murga curry, country chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a koinar saag", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What \"mindful eating\" actually means, practically", "notes": "Mindful eating, practically, just means noticing what''s actually in front of you while you eat — the taste, the textures, roughly how full you''re getting — rather than a formal ritual you have to set aside special time for. It can happen in five minutes as easily as in fifty. Worth knowing: Use commercially prepared bamboo shoots and follow the package instructions. Do not use raw or inadequately prepared fresh shoots.", "breakfast_a": "Pittha, steamed rice dumplings (4 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana ghugni, spiced black-chickpea curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bamboo shoot curry", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, country-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bamboo shoot curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Noticing fullness partway through a meal", "notes": "Fullness builds gradually during a meal, and eating quickly or while distracted makes it easy to eat past the point you''d otherwise have stopped. Pausing partway through — even just once — to notice how hungry you still are can be a useful, low-effort check-in, not a rule to follow at every meal.", "breakfast_a": "Chilka roti, rice-and-lentil crepe (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Jharkhandi-style toor dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a potato-and-pumpkin curry", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, river-fish style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a potato-and-pumpkin curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Screens and phones specifically", "notes": "Phones and screens are worth calling out specifically: several studies link phone use during meals to reduced awareness and recall of what was eaten. Putting the phone out of reach for even one meal a day is a reasonable, realistic place to start — not an all-or-nothing rule.", "breakfast_a": "Dudhauri, milk-and-rice-flour dumplings (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Moong-masoor dal, Jharkhandi-style mixed lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked mixed dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi bhaja", "lunch_b": "Desi murga curry, country chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi bhaja", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a koinar saag", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Eating on the go still counts as eating", "notes": "Eating on the go, standing at the counter, or between other tasks still counts as a real meal — smaller, more frequent eating nourishes you too, and a freshly served plate isn''t inherently more valid than safely reheated leftovers eaten straight from the container. The aim this week is noticing more often, not achieving a particular eating style every time.", "breakfast_a": "Dhuska with a chana ghugni (2 pieces, 1/2 cup ghugni) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Jharkhandi-style toor dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal) with rice (1 cup cooked), a chana ghugni, and a bhindi bhaja", "lunch_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked), a chana ghugni, and a bhindi bhaja", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one perfect meal", "notes": "No single distracted meal undoes anything. The pattern across a week — noticing your food more often than not, without turning every meal into a project — is what this week is actually about.", "breakfast_a": "Pittha with a til chutney (4 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Jharkhandi-style toor dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki curry", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, country-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a bamboo shoot curry", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Was there one meal this week you actually noticed while eating it — and what made that one different from the rest?", "breakfast_a": "Dhuska, fried rice-and-lentil pancake (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Jharkhandi-style toor dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a koinar saag", "lunch_b": "Desi murga curry, country chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a koinar saag", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'Next week continues this run of weekly themes, each focused on a different piece of your own everyday nourishment.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 114: Your own weight, two years on — and why crash dieting usually backfires (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   114,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Your own weight, two years on — and why crash dieting usually backfires',
   '"Whatever your body looks like two years into motherhood, crash dieting is rarely the sustainable answer — the evidence on weight cycling is consistent on that point, whether or not weight loss itself is one of your goals."',
   'Two years on, your body''s shape, weight and composition may be different from before pregnancy in ways that are permanent, gradual, or still settling — there''s no fixed timeline and no single expected outcome. Choosing to work toward a weight-related goal is entirely reasonable; choosing not to have one is equally reasonable. Neither choice needs justifying to anyone. What the evidence speaks to more clearly is method: crash diets and severe, unsupervised restriction are consistently linked to weight cycling, difficulty sustaining any change, and disrupted eating patterns — while a gradual, adequate approach, whether or not it includes an intentional energy reduction, tends to be more sustainable and supports steadier energy and mood.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you''re considering a structured weight-management plan: a doctor or registered dietitian can help build one suited to your own health — a sustainable approach often involves a modest, gradual energy reduction while preserving protein, fibre and other essential nutrients, rather than a restrictive plan found online. This is worth raising at your next appointment if it''s something you want. If you''re pregnant again: intentional weight loss isn''t recommended during pregnancy — focus on nourishing, adequate meals instead, and raise any concerns with your maternity team. If you''re currently breastfeeding: any weight-management approach is worth considering individually with your care team, since breastfeeding increases your energy and nutrient needs compared to before pregnancy. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Manipuri cooking (kangshoi, singju, chak-hao kheer, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Two years into motherhood, your body may or may not look or feel the way it did before pregnancy — there''s no fixed timeline for that, and no single \"right\" outcome to expect. Whatever your own relationship with your weight looks like right now, crash dieting or severe, unsupervised restriction usually isn''t the sustainable way to approach it.", "breakfast_a": "Utongba, black-eyed bean curry with rice (1 cup curry, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kangshoi, mixed vegetable stew (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal or beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a singju", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Manipuri-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a singju", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Why crash diets and severe restriction usually backfire", "notes": "Crash diets and severe, unsupervised restriction are consistently linked in research to weight cycling — losing weight, regaining it, and often more — along with disrupted eating patterns and difficulty sustaining any change long-term. This is different from a balanced, individually appropriate weight-management approach guided by a professional, which can be sustainable when it preserves adequate nutrition rather than cutting back sharply. Worth knowing: Use commercially prepared bamboo shoots and follow the package instructions. Do not use raw or inadequately prepared fresh shoots.", "breakfast_a": "Chak-hao kheer, black rice pudding (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Utongba, black-eyed bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked black-eyed beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bamboo shoot curry", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Manipuri-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bamboo shoot curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Sustainable, adequate eating supports steadier energy", "notes": "Sustainable, adequate eating — regular meals, enough protein and fibre, not skipping one meal to compensate for another — tends to support steadier energy and mood than crash dieting or severe restriction, which can leave you both hungrier and more preoccupied with food. This applies whether or not weight itself is part of your own goals.", "breakfast_a": "Singju, mixed vegetable-and-herb salad with a measured 1/2 cup cooked black-eyed beans and rice (1 cup salad, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kangshoi, mixed vegetable stew (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal or beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a soybean curry", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Manipuri-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a soybean curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If weight loss is a goal for you, here''s how to approach it safely", "notes": "If weight loss is a goal for you, that''s entirely yours to have — wanting one isn''t something to feel apologetic about, any more than not wanting one is. If you''re not pregnant, a sustainable approach can involve a modest, gradual energy reduction while still including enough protein, fibre and other essential nutrients — not skipping meals or cutting out food groups. This series isn''t a prescribed weight-loss plan; working with your doctor or a registered dietitian to build one suited to you tends to go better and last longer than a restrictive plan found online. If you''re pregnant again, intentional weight loss isn''t recommended — focus on nourishing meals instead. If you''re breastfeeding, any weight-management approach is worth considering individually with your care team, since your energy needs are higher than before pregnancy.", "breakfast_a": "Paknam, steamed rice-and-vegetable parcel (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kangshoi, mixed vegetable stew (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal or beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a potato curry", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Manipuri-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a potato curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a bamboo shoot curry", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Body changes at two years — what''s typical, what''s worth asking about", "notes": "Some physical changes two years on are genuinely typical and settle in their own time; others are worth mentioning to your doctor rather than assuming they''ll resolve on their own — persistent pain, a new lump, or a marked and unexplained change are all reasonable things to raise rather than wait out.", "breakfast_a": "Rice with a morok metpa chutney and a measured 1/2 cup cooked soybeans (1 cup rice, 2 tbsp chutney) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kangshoi, mixed vegetable stew (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal or beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a utongba", "lunch_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a utongba", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one number", "notes": "No single number on a scale, on any single day, defines how this week went. The pattern that actually matters is regular, adequate eating across the week — sustainable in a way a crash diet usually isn''t.", "breakfast_a": "Chamthong, mixed vegetable stew with a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal and rice (1 cup stew, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Soybean curry, Manipuri-style (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked soybeans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin curry", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Manipuri-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a soybean curry", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Setting any number aside for a moment, how did your body actually feel this week — energised, tired, steady — and does that tell you anything worth acting on?", "breakfast_a": "Utongba, black-eyed bean curry with rice (1 cup curry, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kangshoi, mixed vegetable stew (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal or beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a singju", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Manipuri-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a singju", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'This closes the current run of weekly themes. Whatever comes next in this series, the idea underneath all of it stays the same: your own nourishment deserves the same attention as everyone else''s at the table.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
