-- postpartum week 115: Selenium — a nutrient your thyroid needs only in small amounts (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   115,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Selenium — a nutrient your thyroid needs only in small amounts',
   '"Selenium supports thyroid hormone metabolism and your body''s antioxidant defences, but the gap between enough and too much is narrower than for most nutrients — how much you get depends on the amount you eat and how much selenium was in the soil it grew in, so a varied diet across the week is a more dependable approach than any single food or supplement."',
   'Selenium is a trace mineral your body needs in only small amounts, but it plays a real role — supporting thyroid hormone metabolism and working as part of your body''s antioxidant defences. Most people who eat a varied diet get enough without any special effort. Because the gap between an adequate amount and too much is narrower for selenium than for most nutrients, more isn''t automatically better here — a few ordinary foods across the week, rather than a concentrated source like a handful of Brazil nuts every day or an unsupervised supplement, is the more dependable approach.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you have a diagnosed thyroid condition: don''t start a selenium supplement on your own, even though selenium is linked to thyroid function — the evidence isn''t settled enough to self-direct. Raise it with your doctor first. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Malwa/Madhya Pradesh cooking (dal bafla, poha, bhutte ka kees, sabudana khichdi, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Selenium is a trace mineral — your body needs only a small amount of it, but that small amount matters for thyroid hormone metabolism and your body''s own antioxidant defences. Most people get enough from ordinary food without needing to think about it separately.", "breakfast_a": "Poha, Indori-style flattened rice with peanuts and a squeeze of lemon (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dal bafla, Malwa-style wheat dumplings with dal (1 cup dal, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal, with 2 bafla) and a bhutte ki sabzi, spiced corn", "lunch_b": "Bhopali chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhutte ki sabzi, spiced corn", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What selenium actually does in your body", "notes": "Selenium is built into a family of proteins called selenoproteins, including the enzymes that activate and regulate thyroid hormone and others that protect your cells from oxidative damage. A shortfall can affect thyroid function; more isn''t automatically better here, since selenium has one of the narrower safety margins among everyday nutrients.", "breakfast_a": "Sabudana khichdi, tapioca-pearl khichdi with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Malwa dal, mixed lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked mixed dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a palak sabzi", "lunch_b": "Machli curry, Narmada-style river fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a palak sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Brazil nuts and selenium — a little goes a long way", "notes": "Brazil nuts are the most concentrated ordinary food source of selenium there is, which makes them easy to overdo without realising it. Regular sunflower seeds, fish, eggs and whole grains provide selenium too, in smaller, steadier amounts that are easier to keep in a safe range day to day. Worth knowing: Brazil nuts contain highly variable amounts of selenium. They are not a reliable way to measure or supplement your selenium intake.", "breakfast_a": "Khoya-matar paratha (2 paratha, 1 cup peas filling) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Malwa dal, mixed lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked mixed dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an aloo bafla, spiced potato", "lunch_b": "Bhopali chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an aloo bafla, spiced potato", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pistachios (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you take levothyroxine or have a thyroid condition", "notes": "If you take levothyroxine or have a diagnosed thyroid condition, don''t start a selenium supplement on your own. Some research has looked at selenium alongside certain autoimmune thyroid conditions, but the evidence isn''t settled enough to self-direct, and unsupervised supplementation carries its own risk of overdoing a nutrient with a narrow safety margin. Raise it with your doctor if you''re curious, rather than starting one yourself.", "breakfast_a": "Bhutte ka kees, spiced grated corn (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dal bafla, Malwa-style wheat dumplings with dal (1 cup dal, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal, with 2 bafla) and a kadhi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Bhopali-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kadhi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted sunflower seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Getting enough without overdoing it — everyday food sources", "notes": "Fish, eggs, sunflower seeds, whole grains, mushrooms and modest amounts of meat all contribute selenium in smaller, steadier amounts than a Brazil nut does. Eating a varied diet across the week is usually enough on its own, without needing a dedicated selenium-rich meal or a supplement.", "breakfast_a": "Malpua (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Malwa dal, mixed lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked mixed dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mushroom sabzi", "lunch_b": "Bhopali chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mushroom sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "If you''re vegetarian, is selenium harder to get?", "notes": "Plant selenium content depends on how much selenium was in the soil the crop was grown in, which varies by region — so a vegetarian or vegan diet can run lower in selenium in some areas and be entirely adequate in others. If you eat a varied vegetarian diet with whole grains, legumes and a small amount of nuts or seeds, that''s usually enough; there''s no need to add a supplement without a specific reason to think you''re short.", "breakfast_a": "Aloo paratha, spiced potato flatbread (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Malwa dal, mixed lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked mixed dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhutte ki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry, Bhopali-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhutte ki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a mushroom sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Setting supplements aside for a moment, is there one ordinary food on this week''s list you''d actually be glad to keep eating regularly?", "breakfast_a": "Poha, Indori-style flattened rice with peanuts and a squeeze of lemon (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dal bafla, Malwa-style wheat dumplings with dal (1 cup dal, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal, with 2 bafla) and a bhutte ki sabzi, spiced corn", "lunch_b": "Bhopali chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhutte ki sabzi, spiced corn", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'Next week looks at something closer to home: what cooking itself can feel like for you, now that the pace of these early years is starting to shift.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;
